---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.3 — String Traversal: The ScrollCipher &amp; AP String Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.8, 2.10 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> String traversal is one of the
    highest-weight topics on the AP Computer Science A exam. Because <code>charAt()</code> and the
    <code>Character</code> class are <strong>not</strong> on the
    <a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a>, every AP String algorithm
    uses a loop over indices combined with <code>substring(i, i + 1)</code>, <code>indexOf</code>,
    <code>length()</code>, and <code>equals</code>.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Spec-First Workflow (Download Both Files):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/ScrollCipher.java" download>ScrollCipher.java</a> — starter stub where
            you will assemble all four String methods.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/ScrollCipherCheck.java" download>ScrollCipherCheck.java</a> — final
            acceptance verification suite (18 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong> For each method, trace the index boundaries and edge
        cases on paper first, draft the code pieces in the prompts below, assemble them in
        <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>, and test your own tricky inputs (empty strings, 1-char
        strings, negative shifts, chained substrings) <em>before</em> running
        <code>ScrollCipherCheck</code> at the end.
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac ScrollCipher.java ScrollCipherCheck.java
java ScrollCipherCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Spec, Edge Cases &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countAdjacentDoubles(String spell)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static int countAdjacentDoubles(String spell)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Precondition:</strong> <code>spell != null</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Return how many indices <code>i</code> have the same
            character at index <code>i</code> and index <code>i + 1</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>"bookkeeper"</code> &rarr; <code>3</code> (<code>"oo"</code>, <code>"kk"</code>, <code>"ee"</code>).</li>
                <li><code>"aaa"</code> &rarr; <code>2</code> (overlapping pairs at indices <code>0–1</code> and <code>1–2</code>).</li>
                <li><code>"dragon"</code> &rarr; <code>0</code>.</li>
                <li><code>""</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> and <code>"x"</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (no crash!).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: The Lookahead Bounds Crash &amp; Short Strings</h3>
        <p>
            To compare character <code>i</code> with character <code>i + 1</code> using the AP Java
            Quick Reference, you compare <code>spell.substring(i, i + 1)</code> with
            <code>spell.substring(i + 1, i + 2)</code>. Trace this buggy version on paper:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Buggy lookahead loop — trace before writing code:
int count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; spell.length(); i++) {
    if (spell.substring(i, i + 1).equals(spell.substring(i + 1, i + 2))) {
        count++;
    }
}
return count;</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>When <code>spell</code> is <code>"bookkeeper"</code> (length <code>10</code>), what
                is the value of <code>i</code> on the final iteration of
                <code>i &lt; spell.length()</code>, and what second argument gets passed into
                <code>spell.substring(i + 1, i + 2)</code> to cause a
                <code>StringIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>?</li>
            <li>What must the loop bound be whenever your loop body inspects both index
                <code>i</code> and index <code>i + 1</code>, and what happens to that bound when
                <code>spell</code> is <code>""</code> (length <code>0</code>) or <code>"x"</code>
                (length <code>1</code>)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>countAdjacentDoubles</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Safe Lookahead Header):</strong> Write the <code>count</code>
                initialization and the <code>for</code> loop header that visits every valid pair
                <code>(i, i + 1)</code> without crashing on <code>""</code> or <code>"x"</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Substring Pair Comparison):</strong> Write the <code>if</code>
                statement inside the loop using <code>substring</code> and <code>.equals(...)</code>
                (why not <code>==</code>?), plus the counter increment and <code>return</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B into <code>countAdjacentDoubles</code> in
            <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>, then trace <code>"aaa"</code> and <code>"x"</code>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Spec, Edge Cases &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>reverseRunes(String scroll)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static String reverseRunes(String scroll)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Precondition:</strong> <code>scroll != null</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Return a new <code>String</code> containing the characters
            of <code>scroll</code> in reverse order.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong> <code>"dragon" &rarr; "nogard"</code>,
            <code>"A" &rarr; "A"</code>, <code>"" &rarr; ""</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: Backward Traversal Boundaries</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If you walk backward from the end of <code>scroll</code>, why does starting at
                <code>int i = scroll.length()</code> and calling
                <code>scroll.substring(i, i + 1)</code> immediately throw
                <code>StringIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>?</li>
            <li>If your backward loop condition is <code>i &gt; 0</code> instead of
                <code>i &gt;= 0</code>, what exact character is missing from
                <code>reverseRunes("dragon")</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>reverseRunes</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Accumulator &amp; Backward Header):</strong> Write the empty result
                string initialization and the backward <code>for</code> loop header from the last
                valid index down to index <code>0</code> — how many times does it execute when
                <code>scroll</code> is <code>""</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Concatenation &amp; Return):</strong> Write the loop body line that
                appends character <code>i</code> onto your reversed string and the final
                <code>return</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B into <code>reverseRunes</code> in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Spec, Edge Cases &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>shiftUppercaseCipher(String text, int shift)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Using the uppercase alphabet string:
    </p>
    <pre><code>String alpha = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";</code></pre>
    <p>
        For each 1-character substring <code>ch = text.substring(i, i + 1)</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Find its position <code>int pos = alpha.indexOf(ch);</code>.</li>
        <li>If <code>pos == -1</code> (spaces, punctuation, digits, or non-uppercase characters),
            append <code>ch</code> unchanged.</li>
        <li>If <code>pos &gt;= 0</code>, shift the position by <code>shift</code> (which may be
            positive, negative, or larger than <code>26</code>!) wrapped into the range
            <code>0..25</code>, and append the shifted letter from <code>alpha</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>("ATTACK", 1)</code> &rarr; <code>"BUUBDL"</code></li>
                <li><code>("DRAGON Z", 3)</code> &rarr; <code>"GUDJRQ C"</code> (<code>Z</code> wraps to <code>C</code>; space stays <code>" "</code>)</li>
                <li><code>("ABZ!", -2)</code> &rarr; <code>"YZX!"</code> (<code>A</code> wraps backward to <code>Y</code>; <code>!</code> stays <code>"!"</code>)</li>
                <li><code>("HAL", 27)</code> &rarr; <code>"IBM"</code> (<code>shift = 27</code> wraps to <code>+1</code>)</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: How Java's <code>%</code> Operator Handles Negative Numbers</h3>
        <p>
            Suppose <code>ch</code> is <code>"A"</code> (<code>pos = 0</code>) and you are
            decrypting with <code>shift = -2</code>. You want to wrap backward 2 steps to
            <code>"Y"</code> (index <code>24</code>).
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>What does Java evaluate <code>(0 + (-2)) % 26</code> to? (It is <strong>not</strong>
                <code>24</code>!) What happens when you pass that negative value into
                <code>alpha.substring(newPos, newPos + 1)</code>?</li>
            <li>Trace the two-step normalization formula
                <code>int newPos = ((pos + shift) % 26 + 26) % 26;</code> on three cases:
                (a) <code>pos = 0, shift = -2</code>, (b) <code>pos = 25, shift = 3</code>, and
                (c) <code>pos = 7, shift = 27</code> — why do we need the outer <code>% 26</code>
                after adding <code>26</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>shiftUppercaseCipher</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Alphabet, Result &amp; Traversal Header):</strong> Write the setup
                variables (<code>alpha</code>, <code>result</code>) and the forward <code>for</code>
                loop that extracts each 1-character substring <code>ch</code> and looks up
                <code>pos</code> in <code>alpha</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Non-Letter Guard vs. Wrapped Shift):</strong> Write the
                <code>if / else</code> that leaves non-uppercase characters unchanged when
                <code>pos == -1</code>, and otherwise computes <code>newPos</code> and appends the
                shifted letter from <code>alpha</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B with <code>return result;</code> inside
            <code>shiftUppercaseCipher</code> in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP FRQ Synthesis Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>purgeCursedGlyph(String scroll, String glyph)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        A classic AP CS A Free Response question asks you to remove every occurrence of a substring
        <code>glyph</code> from <code>scroll</code> using a <code>while</code> loop and
        <code>indexOf</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static String purgeCursedGlyph(String scroll, String glyph)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Precondition:</strong> <code>scroll != null</code>, <code>glyph != null</code>, <code>glyph.length() &gt; 0</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Repeatedly locate the first occurrence of <code>glyph</code>
            using <code>indexOf</code> and splice it out by concatenating everything <em>before</em>
            the match with everything <em>after</em> the match, until <code>glyph</code> no longer
            appears anywhere in <code>scroll</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>("hexed sword of hexes", "hex")</code> &rarr; <code>"ed sword of es"</code></li>
                <li><code>("ababab", "ab")</code> &rarr; <code>""</code></li>
                <li><code>("booook", "oo")</code> &rarr; <code>"bk"</code> (first removal leaves <code>"book"</code>, which still contains <code>"oo"</code>!)</li>
                <li><code>("fireball", "zzz")</code> &rarr; <code>"fireball"</code></li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: The Chained Formation Effect</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Compare <code>purgeCursedGlyph("boook", "oo")</code> (3 <code>'o'</code>s) versus
                <code>purgeCursedGlyph("booook", "oo")</code> (4 <code>'o'</code>s). Trace each step
                by hand: what does <code>"boook"</code> become after removing <code>"oo"</code> once,
                and why does <code>"booook"</code> require a second loop iteration?</li>
            <li>Suppose a student tries to remove all glyphs in a single left-to-right
                <code>for</code> loop instead of re-checking <code>scroll.indexOf(glyph)</code> in
                a <code>while</code> loop. What happens on <code>purgeCursedGlyph("xaabby", "ab")</code>
                when removing the middle <code>"ab"</code> joins the outer <code>"a"</code> and
                <code>"b"</code> into a brand-new <code>"ab"</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>purgeCursedGlyph</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Loop Condition &amp; Index Lookup):</strong> Write the
                <code>while</code> condition (or <code>idx = scroll.indexOf(glyph)</code> loop) that
                keeps repeating as long as <code>glyph</code> still exists in
                <code>scroll</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Substring Splice &amp; Return):</strong> Write the single assignment
                statement inside the loop that removes the occurrence at <code>idx</code> using
                <code>scroll.substring(0, idx)</code> and
                <code>scroll.substring(idx + glyph.length())</code>, plus the final
                <code>return</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Assemble <code>purgeCursedGlyph</code> in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>. Test all four
            methods in a temporary <code>main</code> (or hand-trace <code>""</code>,
            <code>"ABZ!"</code> with <code>-2</code>, and <code>"booook"</code> with
            <code>"oo"</code>), and <strong>only then</strong> run
            <code>java ScrollCipherCheck</code> to confirm all 18 checks pass.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code> were built from your pieces and self-tested before running the check</li>
            <li><code>java ScrollCipherCheck</code> reports <strong><code>18 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection and code-piece boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/string-loops.html">Loops + Strings</a> — traversal, reversing, substring patterns</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> — String methods on the exam</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
